{"schema_version": 4, "name": "recover-oscillator", "investigation": "discovery-sbml-recovery", "status": "investigating", "title": "Recover an oscillator", "confidence": "Refuted", "question": "From synthetic data of a limit-cycle oscillator (repressilator or Brusselator),\ncan the cycle recover a flat SBML whose topology reproduces the oscillation \u2014\nperiod and amplitude \u2014 as sustained dynamics, not just a point-wise fit over one\nwindow?\n", "hypothesis": "Dynamic structure is the hardest recovery: a model can track one trajectory\nwindow yet fail to sustain the limit cycle. The cycle recovers the oscillation\nonly when it recovers the feedback topology (the repression/autocatalytic loop),\nwhich demands frontier-driven proposal of the right reactions plus parameter\nfitting that preserves the Hopf regime \u2014 the rung most likely to expose capability\ngaps and, if unrecoverable as flat SBML, to trigger the non-recoverability check.\n", "objective": "Recover a flat SBML for a limit-cycle oscillator from synthetic targets and\nverify it sustains oscillation with period/amplitude matching the ground truth.\n", "description": "Fourth and hardest rung. Ground truth is a limit-cycle oscillator (e.g.\nrepressilator \u2014 three-gene cyclic repression \u2014 or the Brusselator). Synthetic\ntargets are multi-period trajectories. The recovered model must reproduce the\noscillation as a stable limit cycle, so trajectory scoring is phase-aware and the\nstructural score targets the feedback loop. This rung most sharply tests the\nreaction-proposer and rate-law-fitter, and is the natural trigger for the\nnon-recoverability detector if a faithful flat-SBML form proves out of reach.\n", "parent_studies": [{"study": "recover-gene-switch", "relation": "builds-on"}], "baseline": [{"name": "baseline", "composite": "viva_sbml_decomposition.composites.recovery.recover_candidate", "params": {"target": "recover-oscillator"}}], "variants": [], "interventions": [], "expected_